I"y$<p>A language model asked to multiply two matrices can write a program and hand it to an external runtime. Now imagine that its next forward pass executes an instruction from that program. The following pass executes another. Eventually, the answer is sitting in memory maintained by the model. The visible chat might look the same, but the division of labor has changed.</p>

<p>Four projects explore that boundary. Percepta builds a WebAssembly executor and later a Python environment with writable memory. qwenGPU adds a small parallel machine to Qwen. qwenLab gives Qwen an internal training lab for smaller transformers. Together, they separate three decisions that are easy to conflate: <strong>who chooses the computation, where it runs, and where its state persists</strong>.</p>

<!--more-->

<h2 id="a-transformer-that-executes-webassembly">A transformer that executes WebAssembly</h2>

<p>In <em>Can LLMs Be Computers?</em>, Percepta compiles C programs to WebAssembly and runs them through an interpreter implemented in transformer weights. The model generates an execution trace whose tokens record operations such as changes to memory and the instruction pointer. Each step retrieves relevant information from previous steps. The authors demonstrate this with programs including a Sudoku solver and a minimum-cost matching algorithm. <a href="https://www.percepta.ai/blog/can-llms-be-computers">Percepta, Can LLMs Be Computers?</a></p>

<p>A growing trace creates a performance problem. Standard full attention examines an increasingly long prefix at each decoding step. Percepta’s executor uses two-dimensional hard-max attention heads, where selecting the best key can be treated as a geometric query over a convex hull. In that structured setting, the authors describe logarithmic lookup time in the trace length in place of a linear scan. The speed claim concerns those lookups under the executor’s constraints. <a href="https://www.percepta.ai/blog/can-llms-be-computers">Percepta, Exponentially Fast Attention</a></p>

